      "problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{6!}{3!}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $6!=6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $3!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6!}{3!} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3!}{3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot \\cancel{3!}}{\\cancel{3!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4}{1} \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$",
